{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011C5Rea4nGEvbj7bBfwXKwM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Qs1PL1PjooLGV8dWKontvg","name":"ToolSearch","input":{"query":"select:mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","max_results":1},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1703,"cache_read_input_tokens":48658,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1703,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01H5X9GAUEw6oPgskzJAS1VF","session_id":"e6e97501-db33-49e4-82d6-a111de115694","uuid":"af6cf779-7bfb-4891-a111-2c14204c315a"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qs1PL1PjooLGV8dWKontvg","content":[{"type":"tool_reference","tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01H5X9GAUEw6oPgskzJAS1VF","session_id":"e6e97501-db33-49e4-82d6-a111de115694","uuid":"1a9b14a0-b050-439a-83fa-5a0a03f34c42","timestamp":"2026-05-21T00:20:22.000Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"aa52c382cfa377f88","tool_use_id":"toolu_01LG3r6sLLj21uQBGn4qcyPw","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":73185,"tool_uses":17,"duration_ms":117809},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"bc30e12c-bf97-4975-97a6-a319b1481fda","session_id":"e6e97501-db33-49e4-82d6-a111de115694"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013AD7o9uh28A1BsFpRULVXq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012nn8GvTBLp8M5pWjYUwZAb","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"\n# Reviewer R1: Methodology Soundness and Experimental Rigor\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper introduces Counterfactual Style Decomposition (CSD), a pipeline that combines LLM-based style rewriting, NLI content verification, and position-controlled pairwise comparison to detect per-axis style bias in LLM judges. The central finding — the \"Likert Blindspot\" — is that standard Likert pointwise scoring systematically fails to detect formality bias that pairwise forced-choice reveals on the same data (median p=0.39 vs. p=0.012 across 9 judge×benchmark combinations). A binarized Likert control confirms this is a measurement granularity issue rather than a statistical test artifact. The paper tests 3 judges × 3 benchmarks, includes an n=500 replication, logistic regression decomposition, NLI threshold sensitivity, rewriter ablation, human NLI validation, and consistency filter analysis. Register generalizes as a second working axis; tone fails the NLI gate as a principled negative control.\n\n## Strengths\n\n**S1. The Likert Blindspot claim is well-supported and practically important.** The systematic comparison across all 9 judge×benchmark combinations, with 0/9 reverse patterns, is convincing. The binarized Likert control (Table 3) is the paper's strongest methodological contribution — it isolates measurement structure from test choice by applying the same sign test to binarized Likert scores, finding that the 43.2% tie rate is the mechanism. This is a clean experimental design that directly addresses the most obvious alternative explanation.\n\n**S2. Unusually thorough robustness checks for a short-cycle empirical paper.** The paper covers NLI threshold sensitivity (Table 6: all thresholds 70%–95% significant, monotonic win rate increase), rewriter ablation (Qwen3-32B vs. Llama-3.1-70B, Fisher p=0.42), consistency filter bias analysis (5 comparisons, all p>0.38), human NLI validation (κ=0.72, FPR=6.9%), and an n=500 replication. Each addresses a specific reviewer concern preemptively.\n\n**S3. Honest positioning of contributions.** C1 is explicitly framed as an application combining known components, with RATE, style transfer, and pairwise methodology properly cited. C3 is labeled \"preliminary.\" The tone negative control provides a principled method boundary rather than being swept under the rug. This calibration of claims to evidence is appreciated.\n\n**S4. The n=500 replication provides strong evidence against the power-deficit explanation.** Likert remains borderline non-significant at p=0.082 even with 5× sample size, while pairwise achieves p<0.001. The five n=100 subblocks (σ=0.026) demonstrate high stability. This directly addresses the \"just need more data\" counterargument.\n\n**S5. Logistic regression decomposition (Table 8) adds genuine analytical value.** The non-significant interaction term (p=0.58) is the key result: formality bias operates independently of whether the formal version is the original text. The CSD-adjusted win rate (63.1%, CI: 57.2%–69.0%) provides practitioners with a concrete corrected estimate.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major). The binarized Likert control is shown for only 1 of 9 combinations.** Table 3 reports binarized Likert results only for GPT-4o × LLMBar. This is the decisive evidence for C2, yet we cannot verify whether the mechanism (43% tie rate driving non-significance) holds across judges and benchmarks. Gemini has the strongest effect (72.4%) — does binarized Likert reach significance there? If so, the \"structural measurement limitation\" claim needs qualification. This is a critical gap: the paper claims C2 holds across 3 judges and 3 benchmarks but only demonstrates the mechanism for 1/9.\n\n**W2 (Major). Statistical independence assumption in the logistic regression is questionable.** The N=459 observations come from paired data (counterfactual pairs evaluated in two orders), creating within-pair dependence. The paper mentions GEE with exchangeable correlation in the decisions log (D018) but the submitted paper uses standard logistic regression without cluster-robust standard errors or GEE. The formality p=0.006 may be anti-conservative. The decisions log notes that original preference crosses the significance boundary when clustering is applied (p=0.048→0.052), suggesting the standard errors matter. The paper should report the clustered/GEE results as primary.\n\n**W3 (Major). The \"same data\" claim for Likert vs. pairwise comparison is misleading.** Pairwise and Likert evaluations are fundamentally different judge interactions — different prompts, different cognitive tasks, different output formats. Saying they use \"the same data\" means the same counterfactual text pairs, but the judge sees entirely different evaluation setups. The paper should more carefully distinguish \"same input texts\" from \"same evaluation context.\" The Likert blindspot could partly reflect that these judges are better calibrated for comparative than absolute judgment — a judge-specific property rather than a universal measurement limitation.\n\n**W4 (Minor). Position-consistency filtering discards ~44% of data with unclear implications for generalizability.** While the consistency filter bias analysis (Section 4.5) shows no differences on observables, this does not rule out selection on unobservables. The retained pairs are those where the judge has a strong, position-independent preference — these may systematically overestimate the true population-level bias. The paper should discuss whether the 69.1% win rate among consistent pairs is an upper bound on the population effect.\n\n**W5 (Minor). Only a single Likert scale (1–10) is tested.** The \"Likert Blindspot\" is framed as a general measurement limitation, but is demonstrated on exactly one scale granularity. A 1–100 continuous scale, or log-probability-based scoring, might capture the sub-integer effect. Without testing alternative pointwise scales, the claim is about \"1–10 integer Likert\" specifically, not about pointwise scoring in general. The Discussion should acknowledge this boundary.\n\n**W6 (Minor). Multiple comparison correction is applied within-judge but not across the full 9-test family.** Holm correction within each judge (3 tests) is less conservative than family-wise correction across all 9 combinations. With 9 sign tests, the expected number of false positives at α=0.05 under the null is 0.45. The 7/9 significant result is still compelling under any reasonable correction, but the paper should report family-wise adjusted p-values for the full 3×3 matrix.\n\n**W7 (Minor). Some bibliography entries have placeholder arXiv IDs (e.g., \"2601.xxxxx\").** This is a formatting issue but signals incomplete verification. The reference-audit skill flagged several entries that cannot be independently verified.\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\nQ1. Can you provide binarized Likert results for all 9 judge×benchmark combinations, or at minimum for Gemini×LLMBar (strongest effect) and Claude×MT-Bench (weakest effect)? What is the tie rate across combinations?\n\nQ2. What happens if you use GEE or pair-clustered bootstrap for the logistic regression? Your decisions log (D018) suggests the original preference coefficient crosses the significance boundary — please report both analyses.\n\nQ3. The monotonic increase in win rate with NLI threshold (Table 6: 69.8%→75.0%) is striking. Could this reflect that higher-NLI pairs are those where the rewriter made more superficial changes, and judges prefer the more \"natural\" (less rewritten) version? This would be an NLI-quality confound rather than a purity effect.\n\nQ4. Have you considered testing with continuous-scale scoring (e.g., asking the judge for a score on a 0–100 scale, or extracting log-probabilities)? This would directly test whether the blindspot is about discrete scales versus about absolute-vs-comparative judgment.\n\nQ5. The direction asymmetry (formal→casual 78.8% vs. casual→formal 54.5% in the pilot) is substantial. The paper attributes ~12.2% to original preference (D010), but an alternative explanation is asymmetric rewrite quality. Can you show that NLI scores are symmetric across directions?\n\n## Missing Experiments / Analyses\n\nM1. **Binarized Likert for all 9 combinations** — essential for the C2 universality claim.\n\nM2. **Clustered standard errors or GEE for the logistic regression** — the paper's own decision log identifies this as a known issue (D018) that was resolved in analysis but not reflected in the submitted results.\n\nM3. **Alternative pointwise scale** (e.g., 1–100 or continuous) to test whether the blindspot is Likert-specific or pointwise-specific.\n\nM4. **Effect size comparison with LC-AlpacaEval** — the paper positions against LC-AlpacaEval but never directly compares. Running CSD on the same data where LC-AlpacaEval reports length bias would strengthen the positioning.\n\nM5. **Cross-axis interaction analysis** — formality and register are tested independently. A joint model testing whether they capture the same or different bias dimensions would strengthen C3.\n\n## Preliminary Score\n\n**4 / 6 (Weak Accept)**\n\nThe Likert Blindspot finding is genuine, well-supported by the binarized Likert control, and practically relevant. The experimental coverage is unusually thorough for this type of empirical study, with 13 distinct analyses addressing foreseeable concerns. The honest positioning of contributions and principled negative control (tone) reflect scientific maturity.\n\nHowever, three issues prevent a stronger score: (1) the binarized Likert control — the paper's most important evidence — is shown for only 1/9 combinations, leaving the universality of the mechanism undemonstrated; (2) the logistic regression ignores within-pair dependence, and the paper's own analysis log shows this affects significance of a key coefficient; (3) the \"same data\" framing conflates same input texts with same evaluation setup, and the paper does not test whether alternative pointwise scales (finer granularity, continuous) would close the gap. Addressing W1 and W2 in revision would likely push this to a solid Accept. The paper is suitable for EMNLP 2026 Main contingent on these revisions, and is a clear Findings-level contribution as-is.\n","project":"causal_style_decomp_judge","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260520T233719","overall_score":"4/6 Weak Accept"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":749,"cache_read_input_tokens":71967,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":749,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LG3r6sLLj21uQBGn4qcyPw","session_id":"e6e97501-db33-49e4-82d6-a111de115694","uuid":"29600070-2ba8-42d7-88c7-dc2e4376b98f"}
