{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LG3r6sLLj21uQBGn4qcyPw","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept)** — suitable for EMNLP 2026 Main with revisions, clear Findings-level as-is.\n\n**Top strengths:**\n- The Likert Blindspot claim is well-supported: 7/9 combinations significant for pairwise, 0/9 for Likert, 0/9 reverse pattern\n- Binarized Likert control (Table 3) cleanly isolates measurement granularity from test-choice confound\n- Unusually thorough robustness battery (13 analyses), honest claim calibration, principled negative control (tone)\n- n=500 replication kills the power-deficit counterargument (Likert still p=0.082)\n\n**Three issues preventing a stronger score:**\n1. **W1 (Major):** Binarized Likert control shown for only 1/9 combinations — the decisive C2 evidence needs to be demonstrated across the full matrix, especially for Gemini (strongest effect) where it might actually reach significance\n2. **W2 (Major):** Logistic regression (Table 8) ignores within-pair dependence; the project's own analysis log (D018) shows the original preference coefficient crosses the significance boundary (p=0.048→0.052) under GEE — the paper should report clustered results\n3. **W3 (Major):** \"Same data\" framing conflates same input texts with same evaluation context; no alternative pointwise scales (1–100, continuous) are tested to determine whether the blindspot is specific to discrete integer Likert or general to all absolute scoring\n\n**Actionable path to Accept:** Extending the binarized Likert analysis to all 9 combinations (W1) and reporting GEE-corrected logistic regression (W2) are both zero/low-cost reanalyses of existing data. These two fixes would likely push the score to 5/6."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aa52c382cfa377f88 (use SendMessage with to: 'aa52c382cfa377f88' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 75969\ntool_uses: 17\nduration_ms: 132503</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e6e97501-db33-49e4-82d6-a111de115694","uuid":"881a3d02-6078-49e7-94fb-e5a712b609de","timestamp":"2026-05-21T00:21:25.128Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus: **methodology soundness and experimental rigor**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\"\n\nThe paper proposes Counterfactual Style Decomposition (CSD), a pipeline for detecting per-axis style bias in LLM judges. Key finding: standard Likert pointwise scoring misses style bias that pairwise comparison detects on the same data (\"Likert Blindspot\"). Tested across 3 judges (GPT-4o, Claude-3.5-Sonnet, Gemini-2.0-Flash) × 3 benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench) with formality as primary axis.\n\n## Paper Details (from the 9-page PDF)\n\n### Method (Section 3): CSD Pipeline has 4 stages:\n1. **Counterfactual Style Generation**: Given source text x and target style axis with poles s+/s-, generate counterfactual pair (x+, x-) using Qwen3-32B (Llama-3.1-70B as robustness control)\n2. **Content Preservation Verification**: Dual gate — NLI (DeBERTa-large-MNLI, bidirectional entailment ≥0.80) + BERTScore (≥0.75). Human validation on 80 pairs: κ=0.72, FPR=6.9%\n3. **Dual-Direction Pairwise Evaluation**: Both presentation orders (A-B, B-A) for position control. Only position-consistent pairs analyzed. Sign test against 50%.\n4. **Measurement Comparison**: Same pairs evaluated under Pairwise (forced-choice), Likert (1-10 pointwise, ATE), and Binarized Likert (sign test on score comparison)\n\n### Main Results (Section 4):\n- **Table 1**: 3×3 formality pairwise win rates. Mean 66.4%, 7/9 significant at p<0.05. Judge gradient: Gemini (70.1%) > GPT-4o (67.0%) > Claude (61.7%). Single borderline: Claude×MT-Bench (p=0.065).\n- **Table 2 (Likert Blindspot)**: All 9 Likert ATEs non-significant (all p>0.14), while 7/9 pairwise significant. Median Likert p=0.39 vs median pairwise p=0.012. 0/9 reverse pattern.\n- **Table 3 (Binarized Likert)**: Same sign test on binarized Likert → 58.0% formal rate, p=0.24 (not significant). 43.2% tie rate is the mechanism. Stricter threshold (diff≥2): n=18, p=0.48.\n- **Table 4 (n=500 replication)**: Gemini×LLMBar. WR=66.2%, p<0.001. Likert still p=0.082. Five n=100 subblocks: σ=0.026.\n- **Table 5 (Original preference control)**: Mean 53.0%, all p>0.28. Casual-original subset: Claude 63.0% (p=0.072), Gemini 61.1% (p=0.096).\n- **Table 6 (NLI threshold sensitivity)**: All thresholds 70%-95% yield significant results. Win rate monotonically increases 69.8%→75.0%.\n- **Table 7 (Axis generalization)**: Register axis also works (mean 66.5%, 3/3 judges significant, 0/3 Likert significant). Tone fails NLI gate (65% pass rate).\n- **Table 8 (Logistic regression)**: N=459. Formality β=0.52, p=0.006, OR=1.68. Original preference OR=1.40, p=0.046. Interaction p=0.58 (not significant). CSD-adjusted win rate: 63.1% (CI: 57.2%-69.0%).\n\n### Contributions claimed:\n- C1 (application): CSD pipeline combining known components\n- C2 (primary): Likert Blindspot — structural measurement limitation\n- C3 (preliminary): Axis generalization — formality/register work, tone doesn't\n- C4 (practical): CSD-adjusted win rates via logistic regression\n\n### Limitations acknowledged:\n- Only 2 working style axes\n- LLM-generated counterfactuals + NLI verification may have systematic artifacts\n- n=100 per cell limits precision (n=500 replication helps)\n- CSD-adjusted win rate is simple correction, not full debiasing\n\n## Experiment Coverage Check (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe imagined experiment plan had 14 experiments. The paper covers:\n- exp-001 (multi-judge formality pairwise): ✓ Table 1\n- exp-002 (multi-benchmark formality pairwise): ✓ Table 1\n- exp-003 (Likert vs Pairwise sensitivity): ✓ Table 2\n- exp-004 (Tone negative control): ✓ Section 4.6, Appendix A\n- exp-005 (RATE original preference control): ✓ Table 5, Section 4.5\n- exp-006 (n=500 replication): ✓ Table 4\n- exp-008 (Register axis): ✓ Table 7, Appendix A\n- exp-009 (Rewriter ablation): ✓ Section 4.5, Appendix A\n- exp-010 (Binarized Likert sign test): ✓ Table 3 — CRITICAL, fully covered\n- exp-011 (NLI threshold sensitivity): ✓ Table 6\n- exp-012 (Consistency filter bias): ✓ Section 4.5\n- exp-013 (Human NLI validation): ✓ Section 3.2 + Appendix A\n- exp-014 (Logistic regression): ✓ Table 8\n\nCoverage: 13/13 experiments present (exp-007 not in plan). Excellent coverage.\n\n## Your Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the CSD pipeline well-designed? Are the statistical tests appropriate? Are there confounds not addressed?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are the experiments sufficient to support the claims? Is the evidence for the \"Likert Blindspot\" convincing? Are robustness checks adequate?\n3. **Statistical concerns**: n=100 per cell with ~50% discard rate → effective n≈50. Is this sufficient? Are multiple comparisons properly handled? Is the logistic regression model well-specified?\n4. **Potential weaknesses**: Position-consistency filtering discards ~44% of data. The binarized Likert control is only shown for GPT-4o×LLMBar (1/9 combinations). The \"Likert Blindspot\" claim is about measurement structure but the paper uses a single Likert scale (1-10).\n\nProduce a structured review with: Summary, Strengths (numbered), Weaknesses (numbered with severity: major/minor), Questions for Authors, Missing Experiments/Analyses, and a preliminary score (1-6). Be rigorous but fair. ~800-1200 words.","agentId":"aa52c382cfa377f88","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept)** — suitable for EMNLP 2026 Main with revisions, clear Findings-level as-is.\n\n**Top strengths:**\n- The Likert Blindspot claim is well-supported: 7/9 combinations significant for pairwise, 0/9 for Likert, 0/9 reverse pattern\n- Binarized Likert control (Table 3) cleanly isolates measurement granularity from test-choice confound\n- Unusually thorough robustness battery (13 analyses), honest claim calibration, principled negative control (tone)\n- n=500 replication kills the power-deficit counterargument (Likert still p=0.082)\n\n**Three issues preventing a stronger score:**\n1. **W1 (Major):** Binarized Likert control shown for only 1/9 combinations — the decisive C2 evidence needs to be demonstrated across the full matrix, especially for Gemini (strongest effect) where it might actually reach significance\n2. **W2 (Major):** Logistic regression (Table 8) ignores within-pair dependence; the project's own analysis log (D018) shows the original preference coefficient crosses the significance boundary (p=0.048→0.052) under GEE — the paper should report clustered results\n3. **W3 (Major):** \"Same data\" framing conflates same input texts with same evaluation context; no alternative pointwise scales (1–100, continuous) are tested to determine whether the blindspot is specific to discrete integer Likert or general to all absolute scoring\n\n**Actionable path to Accept:** Extending the binarized Likert analysis to all 9 combinations (W1) and reporting GEE-corrected logistic regression (W2) are both zero/low-cost reanalyses of existing data. These two fixes would likely push the score to 5/6."}],"totalDurationMs":132503,"totalTokens":75969,"totalToolUseCount":17,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2790,"cache_read_input_tokens":72716,"output_tokens":462,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2790},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":462,"cache_read_input_tokens":72716,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2790,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2790,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":9}}}
