{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01V1HKemJf8V31Hvom6HiC5x","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NJqtic484gjNS7mBuTTW8A","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: \"The Likert Blindspot\"\n\n**dream_id:** 20260520T233719 | **review_mode:** full | **date:** 2026-05-21\n\n---\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nThe paper proposes Counterfactual Style Decomposition (CSD), a pipeline combining LLM-based style rewriting, NLI content verification, and position-controlled pairwise comparison to detect per-axis style bias in black-box LLM judges. The central empirical finding (\"Likert Blindspot\") is that standard Likert pointwise scoring fails to detect formality bias (0/9 judge×benchmark combinations significant) while pairwise comparison on the same counterfactual data detects it reliably (7/9 significant, mean win rate 66.4%). A binarized Likert control isolates this as a measurement granularity problem rather than a statistical test artifact. A logistic regression decomposes formality from original-preference confounds (OR=1.68, p=0.006). Register replicates the pattern; tone fails the NLI gate, providing a principled method boundary. 9 pages, ~25 references.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer Panel\n\n- **R1** — Methods & Experimental Rigor\n- **R2** — Novelty, Positioning & Related Work\n\n---\n\n## Consensus Strengths\n\n**S1. The Likert Blindspot is well-isolated and practically important.** The 0/9 vs 7/9 contrast across a full 3×3 matrix is striking. The 30× median p-value gap (0.39 vs 0.012) is hard to dismiss. No reverse pattern exists (0/9 cases where Likert detects bias that pairwise misses). Both reviewers flagged this as the strongest contribution. [R1, R2]\n\n**S2. The binarized Likert control (Table 3) is a clever experimental design.** By applying the same sign test to binarized Likert scores, the paper cleanly separates the measurement-structure explanation from the test-choice explanation. This is the paper's most methodologically sophisticated contribution. [R1, R2]\n\n**S3. Unusually thorough robustness battery.** 13 analyses covering: n=500 replication, NLI threshold sensitivity, rewriter ablation (Qwen3 vs Llama), consistency filter bias, human NLI validation (κ=0.72), original preference control (RATE), and logistic regression decomposition. The n=500 replication where Likert remains non-significant (p=0.082) while pairwise reaches p<0.001 effectively kills the power-deficit counterargument. [R1, R2]\n\n**S4. Honest contribution framing.** C1 explicitly acknowledged as application (combining known components), C3 as preliminary. This calibration builds trust. [R2]\n\n**S5. Principled method boundary from negative control.** The tone axis failure, analyzed mechanistically (epistemic commitment changes violate NLI, 30% catastrophic failures), is more informative than a third positive result. Gives practitioners a clear decision criterion. [R1, R2]\n\n---\n\n## Consensus Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n**W1. Binarized Likert control shown for only 1/9 combinations (GPT-4o×LLMBar).** [R1]\nThis is the paper's most decisive evidence for C2, yet it appears only for a single cell of the 3×3 matrix. Gemini×LLMBar (72.4% pairwise win rate) is the strongest-effect combination — if binarized Likert *also* reaches significance there, the \"structural measurement limitation\" claim weakens. The paper must show this control for at minimum the 3 LLMBar combinations (all judges), ideally all 9. This is a zero-cost reanalysis of existing data.\n**Severity: Major. Actionable: yes, zero-cost.**\n\n**W2. \"Causal\" language overclaimed.** [R2]\nThe paper uses \"causal\" extensively (method name \"Counterfactual Style Decomposition,\" \"independent causal effect,\" \"causal factor\"). However: no DAG, no do-calculus, no SUTVA discussion, no sensitivity analysis for unmeasured confounding. The logistic regression (Table 8) controls for measured confounds but is associational, not causal. The counterfactual rewriting + NLI verification design provides strong *interventional* evidence, but formal causal identification requires more scaffolding. Should either (a) provide a minimal DAG with stated assumptions, or (b) soften to \"interventional\" / \"quasi-experimental.\"\n**Severity: Major. Actionable: yes, framing change.**\n\n**W3. Known psychometric limitation being confirmed — novelty debatable.** [R2]\nJamieson (2004), Thurstone (1927), and Karpinska et al. (2021) — all cited — already document Likert insensitivity and forced-choice superiority. The mechanism (measurement granularity, tied scores, scale resolution) was predicted by existing theory. The contribution is confirming this in the LLM-judge-style-bias setting with specific quantification. The paper should more forthrightly frame the novelty as *empirical demonstration in a high-stakes applied context + quantification of the gap magnitude*, rather than presenting the Likert Blindspot as a discovery.\n**Severity: Major. Actionable: yes, framing change.**\n\n**W4. Logistic regression ignores within-pair dependence.** [R1]\nTable 8's logistic regression pools N=459 observations from pairs evaluated in both directions, but does not account for within-pair clustering. The original preference coefficient (p=0.046) is borderline — under GEE or clustered standard errors, it may flip to non-significant, which would change the decomposition narrative. The paper should report both naive and clustered results.\n**Severity: Major. Actionable: yes, zero-cost reanalysis.**\n\n**W5. Insufficient differentiation from RATE [Feuer et al. 2025] and Zhou et al. [2025].** [R2]\nRATE already provides the double-rewrite ATE framework. Zhou et al. already applied counterfactual evaluation to LLM judges. The related work gives Zhou et al. a single sentence. The paper's delta is: (a) applying to style axes, and (b) the Likert-vs-pairwise comparison. Only (b) is substantively new. Needs a clearer articulation (comparison table or paragraph) of what each prior method can/cannot do.\n**Severity: Major. Actionable: yes, writing change.**\n\n**W6. No alternative pointwise scales tested.** [R1]\nThe paper tests only a discrete 1–10 Likert scale. Without testing continuous scales (1–100, slider, log-probability), we cannot determine whether the blindspot is specific to coarse discrete scales or inherent to all absolute scoring. This limits the generalizability of C2's core claim (\"Likert scoring structurally underestimates\").\n**Severity: Major. Harder to fix (requires new experiments).**\n\n### Minor\n\n**W7. Placeholder references.** [R2]\nChen et al. [2026] (\"2601.xxxxx\"), Lu et al. [2025] CASSE (\"2501.xxxxx\"), Saito et al. [2023] (\"2310.xxxxx\"), Zhou et al. [2025] (\"2502.xxxxx\") all have placeholder arXiv IDs. If these are real papers, actual IDs must be provided. If fabricated, this is a serious integrity issue.\n**Severity: Minor (fixable), but potentially escalates if references are not real.**\n\n**W8. 44% discard rate from position-consistency filtering.** [R1, R2]\nWhile no systematic bias is demonstrated in retained vs. discarded pairs (5 tests, all p>0.38), discarding nearly half the data means CSD characterizes bias only for the subset of inputs where judges are position-consistent. This may exclude precisely the contested cases where style bias operates most. Deserves more discussion.\n**Severity: Minor.**\n\n**W9. SOS-Bench positioning slightly unfair.** [R2]\nThe paper frames SOS-Bench as \"correlational,\" which understates their injection methodology (controlled defect injection with 152K data points is itself interventional). Should acknowledge SOS-Bench's strengths more fairly.\n**Severity: Minor.**\n\n**W10. Modest effect size with thin practical guidance.** [R2]\nCSD-adjusted formality bias is ~13pp. The practical correction mechanism (subtract original preference estimate) is rudimentary compared to LC-AlpacaEval's regression framework. C4 amounts to four bullet points.\n**Severity: Minor.**\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. Can you provide actual arXiv IDs for all four placeholder references? (W7)\n2. What does the binarized Likert control look like for Gemini×LLMBar (strongest effect)? If binarized Likert is significant there, how does that affect the C2 claim? (W1)\n3. Given McFadden R²=0.042, how should readers interpret the significant formality OR? The model explains very little variance. (W4 related)\n4. The direction asymmetry (formal→original 78.8% vs casual→original 54.5% in MVP) is substantial. Does this suggest the \"formality bias\" partly reflects rewriting-quality asymmetry? (R2)\n5. Have you considered GEE or mixed-effects logistic regression to account for within-pair clustering? (W4)\n6. Would the Likert Blindspot persist with a 1–100 continuous scale or with judge log-probabilities? (W6)\n\n---\n\n## Missing References (R2)\n\n- Ye et al. (2024), \"FLASK\" — multi-dimensional LLM evaluation\n- Stureborg et al. (2024), \"Large Language Models are Inconsistent and Biased Evaluators\"\n- Chiang & Lee (2023), \"Can Large Language Models Be an Alternative to Human Evaluations?\"\n- Li et al. (2024), \"Split and Merge\" — position bias mitigation\n\n---\n\n## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nThe review_context.yaml lists 13 imagined experiments. The paper covers all 13:\n- exp-001 through exp-006, exp-008 through exp-014: all present in tables or appendix\n- Coverage: **13/13 (100%)**. Excellent.\n\nThe paper's experimental program is comprehensive and well-organized. The main gap is not missing experiments but missing *analyses on existing data* (binarized Likert across full matrix, GEE correction).\n\n---\n\n## Scores\n\n| Reviewer | Score | Confidence |\n|----------|-------|------------|\n| R1 (Methods) | 4/6 (Weak Accept) | 4/5 |\n| R2 (Novelty) | 4/6 (Weak Accept) | 4/5 |\n| **Consolidated** | **4/6 (Weak Accept)** | **4/5** |\n\n---\n\n## Meta-Assessment\n\n**Borderline Main / strong Findings.** The paper presents a well-executed empirical study with a practically important finding (Likert scoring misses style bias) backed by thorough robustness checks and 100% experiment coverage. The binarized Likert control is clever and the 3×3 matrix provides convincing cross-validation.\n\nHowever, three structural issues hold it at the Main threshold boundary:\n1. The core finding confirms a known psychometric principle in a new domain — valuable but incremental\n2. The decisive binarized Likert evidence appears for only 1/9 combinations\n3. Causal language exceeds the formal identification support\n\n**Clear path to 5/6 (Accept):** Extend binarized Likert to all 9 combinations, report GEE-corrected logistic regression, soften causal language to \"interventional,\" and more explicitly differentiate from RATE/Zhou. All are low-cost changes on existing data. If the binarized Likert pattern holds across all 9 combinations — which the evidence suggests it will — the C2 claim becomes substantially stronger.\n\n**Risk factor:** If binarized Likert reaches significance for Gemini (strongest effect at 72.4%), the \"structural limitation\" narrative weakens to \"scale-dependent limitation,\" which is still interesting but narrower than claimed.\n\n**Overall recommendation: Revise and resubmit (lean accept).** The core insight is sound, the execution is thorough, and the fixes are straightforward. With the identified revisions, this paper should clear the Main conference bar.","project":"causal_style_decomp_judge","tier":"best","dream_id":"20260520T233719","overall_score":"4/6 Weak Accept — borderline Main, strong Findings","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2217,"cache_read_input_tokens":76060,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2217},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e6e97501-db33-49e4-82d6-a111de115694","uuid":"f11c8567-f95a-44b5-816a-a6e2b7f440cc"}
